window.CELPIP_GRAMMAR = [{"id":"G-01","topic":"Verb tenses (past and present perfect)","lesson":"Use the simple past for a finished action at a stated or clear time in the past. Use the present perfect (have or has plus the past participle) when the time is not stated, or when a past action still matters now. Time markers help: yesterday, last week and in 2019 call for the simple past, while already, yet, ever, never, since and for usually call for the present perfect. Example: I moved to Calgary in 2019. Example: I have lived here for six years.","items":[{"q":"She ___ the report yesterday afternoon.","options":["has finished","finished","have finished","has been finishing"],"answer":1,"why":"Yesterday afternoon is a finished time, so the simple past is needed."},{"q":"I ___ in this city since 2021.","options":["lived","did live","am living","have lived"],"answer":3,"why":"Since plus a starting point links the past to now, which needs the present perfect."},{"q":"___ you ever taken a ferry to Vancouver Island?","options":["Did","Do","Have","Were"],"answer":2,"why":"Ever and the participle taken form a present perfect question with have."},{"q":"The bus ___ yet, so we are still waiting.","options":["hasn't arrived","didn't arrive","wasn't arriving","hadn't arrived"],"answer":0,"why":"Yet with a situation that continues now calls for the present perfect."},{"q":"When I was a student, I ___ at a café every weekend.","options":["have worked","am working","worked","work"],"answer":2,"why":"When I was a student sets a finished period, so the simple past fits."},{"q":"He ___ in Halifax for ten years before he moved to Ottawa.","options":["has lived","lives","is living","lived"],"answer":3,"why":"The stay in Halifax ended when he moved, so it is a finished past."}]},{"id":"G-02","topic":"Future forms","lesson":"Use will for quick decisions made as you speak and for general predictions. Use be going to for plans already made and for predictions based on evidence you can see now. Use the present continuous for arranged events with a set time, and the present simple for timetables. Use will have plus a participle for something finished by a future point. Example: I'll answer the door. Example: We are going to repaint the hall on Saturday.","items":[{"q":"The phone is ringing. I ___ it.","options":["'ll get","would get","have gotten","was going to get"],"answer":0,"why":"A decision made at the moment of speaking takes will."},{"q":"The film ___ at 7:30 tonight; the poster says so.","options":["has started","started","starts","is going to be started"],"answer":2,"why":"A fixed schedule is expressed with the present simple."},{"q":"I've bought the paint, so this weekend I ___ the bedroom.","options":["painted","have painted","paint","am going to paint"],"answer":3,"why":"A plan already made takes be going to."},{"q":"By the end of this year, I ___ in this job for exactly three years.","options":["will work","have worked","will have worked","worked"],"answer":2,"why":"By a future point, a period that will be complete uses the future perfect."},{"q":"Don't call at 8 tonight. I ___ my sister at that time.","options":["visited","will be visiting","have visited","visit"],"answer":1,"why":"An action in progress at a future time uses the future continuous."},{"q":"If it ___ tomorrow, we will cancel the picnic.","options":["will rain","would rain","is going to rain","rains"],"answer":3,"why":"After if, a future meaning is expressed with the present simple."}]},{"id":"G-03","topic":"Articles","lesson":"Use a or an before a singular countable noun that is new or not specific; use an before a vowel sound. Use the when the listener knows which one you mean, when it was mentioned already, or when only one exists. Use no article for plural or uncountable nouns in a general sense, and for most languages, meals and names. Example: I saw a bear; the bear ran away. Example: Honesty matters, and I study French.","items":[{"q":"She bought a laptop and a phone. ___ laptop was on sale.","options":["A","The","An","No article"],"answer":1,"why":"The laptop was already mentioned, so the speaker uses the."},{"q":"My brother is ___ engineer.","options":["a","the","no article","an"],"answer":3,"why":"Engineer begins with a vowel sound, so an is used."},{"q":"___ honesty is important in any workplace.","options":["No article","The","A","An"],"answer":0,"why":"An uncountable abstract noun used in general needs no article."},{"q":"Could you close ___ door, please? It is cold in here.","options":["a","an","no article","the"],"answer":3,"why":"Both people know which door is meant, so the is used."},{"q":"The sun rises in ___ east.","options":["the","a","an","no article"],"answer":0,"why":"Compass directions used as places take the."},{"q":"She studied ___ French before moving to Montréal.","options":["the","a","an","no article"],"answer":3,"why":"Names of languages take no article."}]},{"id":"G-04","topic":"Prepositions of time and place","lesson":"For time, use at with clock times and points (at 9:30, at noon), on with days and dates (on Friday, on May 3), and in with months, years, seasons and parts of the day (in March, in 2020, in the evening). Use until for a state continuing to a time and by for a deadline. For place, use at for a point, on for a surface, and in for an enclosed area. Example: We meet at noon on Tuesday. Example: The keys are in the drawer.","items":[{"q":"The meeting starts ___ 9:30 a.m.","options":["at","on","in","by"],"answer":0,"why":"Clock times take at."},{"q":"My birthday is ___ March.","options":["on","at","of","in"],"answer":3,"why":"Months take in."},{"q":"We have a delivery ___ Friday.","options":["in","at","on","by"],"answer":2,"why":"Days of the week take on."},{"q":"The picture is hanging ___ the wall.","options":["in","at","by","on"],"answer":3,"why":"A surface takes on."},{"q":"I will stay at my desk ___ 6 p.m., then I will go home.","options":["until","by","in","on"],"answer":0,"why":"Until shows a continuing state that stops at a time."},{"q":"They arrived ___ the airport two hours early.","options":["in","to","at","on"],"answer":2,"why":"Arrive at is used for a specific building or point."}]},{"id":"G-05","topic":"Conditionals","lesson":"Zero conditional: if plus present, present, for general truths. First conditional: if plus present, will plus verb, for real future possibilities. Second conditional: if plus past, would plus verb, for unlikely or imaginary situations; use were for all subjects in formal English. Third conditional: if plus past perfect, would have plus participle, for imagined past results. Unless means if not. Example: If I had a car, I would drive. Example: If it rains, we will stay home.","items":[{"q":"If you heat ice, it ___.","options":["will melt","would melt","melted","melts"],"answer":3,"why":"A general truth uses the present simple in both parts."},{"q":"If the weather ___ nice tomorrow, we will go hiking.","options":["will be","is","would be","was"],"answer":1,"why":"A real future possibility puts the present simple after if."},{"q":"If I ___ more free time, I would learn the piano.","options":["have","will have","had","would have"],"answer":2,"why":"An unreal present situation uses the past simple after if."},{"q":"If she had left earlier, she ___ the train.","options":["would catch","had caught","will catch","would have caught"],"answer":3,"why":"An imagined past result uses would have plus the participle."},{"q":"I wouldn't sign that contract if I ___ you.","options":["am","were","will be","would be"],"answer":1,"why":"Giving advice in the second conditional uses were."},{"q":"Unless you ___ the deadline, the application will not be considered.","options":["meet","don't meet","will meet","met"],"answer":0,"why":"Unless already means if not, so the verb stays positive."}]},{"id":"G-06","topic":"Modal verbs","lesson":"Modal verbs come before the base verb and never take -s. Use can and could for ability or requests, may and might for possibility, should for advice, and must or have to for strong obligation. Must not means something is forbidden, but do not have to means it is unnecessary. Must have plus a participle shows a strong guess about the past. Example: You must not smoke here. Example: She might be late.","items":[{"q":"You ___ park here; it's a fire lane.","options":["don't have to","might not","mustn't","needn't to"],"answer":2,"why":"Mustn't expresses a prohibition."},{"q":"You ___ bring lunch; the workshop provides one.","options":["mustn't","can't","shouldn't","don't have to"],"answer":3,"why":"Don't have to means it is not necessary."},{"q":"The lights are off and nobody answers. They ___ have gone home already.","options":["must","ought","need","would"],"answer":0,"why":"Must have plus a participle shows a strong deduction about the past."},{"q":"When I was ten, I ___ swim across the lake.","options":["can","must","could","should"],"answer":2,"why":"Could shows ability in the past."},{"q":"You look exhausted. You ___ take a day off.","options":["can't","mustn't","shall not","should"],"answer":3,"why":"Should gives friendly advice."},{"q":"___ I borrow your pen, please?","options":["Could","Must","Should","Ought"],"answer":0,"why":"Could is a polite way to ask permission."}]},{"id":"G-07","topic":"Linking words and transitions","lesson":"Linking words show how ideas relate. Use because, since and as for reason, so and therefore for result, but, however and although for contrast, and whereas to compare two things. Use in addition and moreover to add. Despite and because of are followed by a noun or an -ing form, not a full clause. However and therefore start a new sentence and take a comma. Example: Despite the rain, we walked. Example: It was late; therefore, we left.","items":[{"q":"___ the heavy snow, the bus arrived on time.","options":["Although","Despite","However","Because"],"answer":1,"why":"Despite is followed by a noun phrase and shows contrast."},{"q":"The apartment is small. ___, it is close to the subway.","options":["Therefore","Because","However","Unless"],"answer":2,"why":"The second sentence contrasts with the first, so however fits."},{"q":"She missed the bus; ___, she was late for the interview.","options":["although","despite","unless","therefore"],"answer":3,"why":"Being late is the result of missing the bus."},{"q":"___ he had little experience, he got the job.","options":["Although","Despite","Because","Therefore"],"answer":0,"why":"Although introduces a full clause that contrasts with the main clause."},{"q":"I enjoy cooking, ___ my sister prefers to order takeout.","options":["because","so","whereas","unless"],"answer":2,"why":"Whereas compares two different people or things."},{"q":"The road is closed ___ construction.","options":["because of","because","although","so that"],"answer":0,"why":"Because of is followed by a noun."}]},{"id":"G-08","topic":"Relative clauses","lesson":"A relative clause adds information about a noun. Use who for people, which for things, that for either in an essential clause, whose for possession, and where for places. Essential clauses have no commas; extra-information clauses use commas and cannot use that. Use whom after a preposition or as an object in formal writing. Example: The tool that broke was old. Example: Ravi, who lives nearby, helped us.","items":[{"q":"The woman ___ lives next door is a nurse.","options":["which","who","whose","where"],"answer":1,"why":"Who refers to a person acting as the subject."},{"q":"This is the laptop ___ I bought last week.","options":["who","whose","that","where"],"answer":2,"why":"That refers to a thing in an essential clause."},{"q":"The coworker ___ car broke down got a ride from me.","options":["whose","who","which","whom"],"answer":0,"why":"Whose shows that the car belongs to the coworker."},{"q":"Toronto, ___ is Canada's largest city, hosts many events.","options":["that","who","where","which"],"answer":3,"why":"Extra information set off by commas uses which, not that."},{"q":"That is the café ___ we first met.","options":["which","where","who","whose"],"answer":1,"why":"Where refers to a place."},{"q":"The candidate to ___ I spoke was very polite.","options":["whom","who","whose","which"],"answer":0,"why":"After a preposition, the object form whom is required."}]},{"id":"G-09","topic":"Comparatives and superlatives","lesson":"Compare two things with a comparative: add -er to short adjectives (taller) and use more before long ones (more careful). Compare three or more with a superlative: the plus -est (the tallest) or the most plus long adjectives (the most careful). Irregular forms include good, better, best and bad, worse, worst. Use than after a comparative, and as ... as for equality. Never double the marking. Example: This chair is more comfortable than that one. Example: She is the fastest runner.","items":[{"q":"This route is ___ than the highway.","options":["more short","shortest","shorter","the shorter"],"answer":2,"why":"A short adjective takes -er before than."},{"q":"She is the ___ person on our team.","options":["more reliable","reliabler","most reliablest","most reliable"],"answer":3,"why":"A long adjective forms the superlative with the most."},{"q":"The second test was not as difficult ___ the first one.","options":["than","as","like","that"],"answer":1,"why":"Equality comparisons use as ... as."},{"q":"Of all the options, the train is ___.","options":["cheaper","the cheapest","more cheap","cheap"],"answer":1,"why":"Of all signals a superlative."},{"q":"My new phone is ___ my old one.","options":["far better than","more good than","gooder than","best than"],"answer":0,"why":"Good has the irregular comparative better, followed by than."},{"q":"The more you practise, the ___ you will become.","options":["most confident","as confident","confidenter","more confident"],"answer":3,"why":"The more ... the more is a paired comparative pattern."}]},{"id":"G-10","topic":"Reported speech","lesson":"When reporting what someone said in the past, the verb usually moves back one tense: present becomes past, will becomes would, can becomes could. Time words change too: now becomes then, tomorrow becomes the next day. Reported questions use statement word order with no do-support, and yes/no questions use if or whether. Use tell with a person and say without one. Example: She said she was tired. Example: He asked if I could help.","items":[{"q":"'I will call you tomorrow,' he said. He said he ___ call me the next day.","options":["will","would","shall","did"],"answer":1,"why":"Will shifts back to would in reported speech."},{"q":"'Where do you live?' she asked me. She asked me where ___.","options":["did I live","I lived","do I live","lived I"],"answer":1,"why":"A reported question uses statement order and a backshifted verb."},{"q":"He ___ me that the flight was delayed.","options":["said","spoke","talked","told"],"answer":3,"why":"Tell is followed directly by a person."},{"q":"'Did you finish the report?' the manager asked. The manager asked ___ I had finished the report.","options":["if","that","what","did"],"answer":0,"why":"A yes/no question is reported with if or whether."},{"q":"'I can help you now,' said Luis. Luis said he ___ help me then.","options":["can","may","could","will"],"answer":2,"why":"Can shifts back to could."},{"q":"'Don't touch the display,' the guard told us. The guard told us ___ the display.","options":["don't touch","to not touching","not touch","not to touch"],"answer":3,"why":"A reported negative command uses not to plus the base verb."}]},{"id":"G-11","topic":"The passive voice","lesson":"Form the passive with a form of be plus the past participle: The report is written, was written, will be written, has been written. The object of the active sentence becomes the subject. Use the passive when the doer is unknown, obvious or unimportant, or when the receiver of the action is the focus. Add by plus the doer only if it matters. Only verbs that take an object can be made passive. Example: The windows were cleaned. Example: The award was won by our team.","items":[{"q":"The invoices ___ every Friday.","options":["send","are sent","are sending","have send"],"answer":1,"why":"Invoices receive the action, so the passive are sent is needed."},{"q":"The bridge ___ in 1962.","options":["built","is built","has built","was built"],"answer":3,"why":"A past completed event in the passive uses was built."},{"q":"The windows ___ at the moment, so the office is noisy.","options":["are repairing","is being repaired","are being repaired","have repair"],"answer":2,"why":"A passive in progress with a plural subject uses are being repaired."},{"q":"The safety rules ___ by every visitor.","options":["must be followed","must follow","must be following","must followed"],"answer":0,"why":"A passive modal uses must be plus the participle."},{"q":"Which sentence is in the passive voice?","options":["The chef prepared the meal.","The meal was prepared by the chef.","The chef was preparing the meal.","The chef has prepared the meal."],"answer":1,"why":"Only the second sentence puts the receiver of the action in the subject position."},{"q":"The results ___ yet.","options":["haven't announced","aren't announcing","haven't been announced","didn't be announced"],"answer":2,"why":"The present perfect passive is have been plus the participle."}]},{"id":"G-12","topic":"Gerunds and infinitives","lesson":"Some verbs are followed by a gerund (the -ing form): enjoy, avoid, finish, suggest, consider, practise. Others take the infinitive (to plus the base verb): decide, plan, hope, agree, refuse, manage, afford. After a preposition, always use a gerund. A few verbs change meaning: remember doing is a memory, while remember to do is a duty. Use the infinitive after adjectives like important. Example: I enjoy running. Example: She decided to apply.","items":[{"q":"She avoided ___ about the mistake.","options":["to talk","talk","talked","talking"],"answer":3,"why":"Avoid is followed by a gerund."},{"q":"They decided ___ the office downtown.","options":["to move","moving","move","moved"],"answer":0,"why":"Decide is followed by an infinitive."},{"q":"I'm interested in ___ a course in project management.","options":["to take","taking","take","took"],"answer":1,"why":"A preposition is followed by a gerund."},{"q":"I remember ___ the door, so I know it is locked.","options":["to lock","lock","locking","locked"],"answer":2,"why":"Remember plus a gerund refers to a memory of an earlier action."},{"q":"It's important ___ your résumé before applying.","options":["updating","update","updated","to update"],"answer":3,"why":"An adjective such as important is followed by an infinitive."},{"q":"We can't afford ___ a new vehicle this year.","options":["buying","to buy","buy","bought"],"answer":1,"why":"Afford is followed by an infinitive."}]},{"id":"G-13","topic":"Phrasal verbs","lesson":"A phrasal verb is a verb plus a particle such as up, out, off or down, and the meaning is often different from the parts. Some can be separated by an object (turn the radio down) and some cannot (look after the children). With a pronoun, separable verbs must be split: turn it down, not turn down it. Learn them in whole phrases. Example: The match was called off. Example: I ran into an old friend.","items":[{"q":"Please ___ the form before you leave.","options":["fill off","fill out","fill away","fill by"],"answer":1,"why":"Fill out means to complete a form."},{"q":"I can't ___ why the shipment is late.","options":["figure up","figure over","figure at","figure out"],"answer":3,"why":"Figure out means to understand or work out."},{"q":"The meeting was ___ until next week because of the storm.","options":["put off","put out","put up","put in"],"answer":0,"why":"Put off means to postpone."},{"q":"We ran ___ of printer paper.","options":["off","away","out","up"],"answer":2,"why":"Run out of means to have none left."},{"q":"She ___ her grandmother; they have the same smile.","options":["takes up","takes after","takes over","takes in"],"answer":1,"why":"Take after means to resemble a family member."},{"q":"Could you ___ the music? I'm trying to concentrate.","options":["turn up","turn into","turn on","turn down"],"answer":3,"why":"Turn down means to lower the volume."}]},{"id":"G-14","topic":"Countable and uncountable nouns","lesson":"Countable nouns can be counted and have a plural: one chair, two chairs. Uncountable nouns cannot, so they have no plural form and take a singular verb: advice, information, furniture, equipment, luggage, news. Use many, few, a number of and fewer with countable nouns, and much, little, an amount of and less with uncountable nouns. To count an uncountable noun, use a piece of or an item of. Example: I need some advice. Example: She packed three pieces of luggage.","items":[{"q":"I need some ___ about the bus schedule.","options":["informations","an information","information","many information"],"answer":2,"why":"Information is uncountable and has no plural."},{"q":"How ___ time do we have before the call?","options":["much","many","few","several"],"answer":0,"why":"Time in this sense is uncountable, so much is used."},{"q":"There are only ___ seats left.","options":["a little","a few","much","an amount of"],"answer":1,"why":"Seats are countable, so a few is correct."},{"q":"She gave me two useful ___.","options":["advices","advice's","many advice","pieces of advice"],"answer":3,"why":"Advice is uncountable, so it is counted with pieces of."},{"q":"The ___ of cars on the highway has increased.","options":["amount","number","much","little"],"answer":1,"why":"Number is used with countable nouns such as cars."},{"q":"We bought new ___ for the living room.","options":["furnitures","a furniture","furniture","many furniture"],"answer":2,"why":"Furniture is an uncountable noun."}]},{"id":"G-15","topic":"Word order and question forms","lesson":"Statements follow subject, verb, object, with place before time at the end: She works downtown on weekdays. Adverbs of frequency go before the main verb but after be. In questions, put the auxiliary before the subject: Where do you work? Questions about the subject use no auxiliary. In indirect questions, return to statement order: Can you tell me where she works? Example: He rarely eats lunch. Example: Who called you?","items":[{"q":"Where ___ every morning?","options":["you go","go you","are you go","do you go"],"answer":3,"why":"A question word is followed by the auxiliary do, then the subject."},{"q":"Could you tell me where ___?","options":["is the station","does the station","the station is","the station does"],"answer":2,"why":"An indirect question uses statement word order."},{"q":"She ___ late for meetings.","options":["always is","is always","does always","always does"],"answer":1,"why":"Adverbs of frequency come after the verb be."},{"q":"Who ___ the last piece of cake?","options":["ate","did eat","did ate","does ate"],"answer":0,"why":"A question about the subject has no auxiliary."},{"q":"He speaks ___.","options":["fluently English","English fluently","fluent English","English fluent"],"answer":1,"why":"The object goes directly after the verb, and the adverb follows it."},{"q":"I wonder ___ the results will be posted.","options":["when will","will when","when","when do"],"answer":2,"why":"Embedded questions take statement order, so will stays after the subject."}]},{"id":"G-16","topic":"Punctuation (commas, apostrophes)","lesson":"Use a comma after an introductory phrase or clause, before a conjunction joining two full sentences (and, but, so), and around extra information. A comma alone cannot join two full sentences. Apostrophes show possession (the manager's desk, the managers' desks) and mark missing letters (it's means it is). Its with no apostrophe is possessive. Never use an apostrophe for a simple plural. Example: After lunch, we left. Example: The dog wagged its tail.","items":[{"q":"Choose the correct sentence.","options":["When the bus arrived, we got on.","When the bus arrived we, got on.","When, the bus arrived we got on.","When the bus, arrived we got on."],"answer":0,"why":"A comma follows the introductory clause."},{"q":"Two sisters share one room. Which phrase is correct?","options":["my sisters room","my sister's room","my sisters's room","my sisters' room"],"answer":3,"why":"A plural noun ending in s shows possession with only an apostrophe."},{"q":"The dog wagged ___ tail.","options":["it's","its'","its","it is"],"answer":2,"why":"Its without an apostrophe is the possessive form."},{"q":"Which sentence is punctuated correctly?","options":["My brother who lives in Regina, is a teacher.","My brother, who lives in Regina, is a teacher.","My brother, who lives in Regina is a teacher.","My brother who, lives in Regina is a teacher."],"answer":1,"why":"Extra information is set off by a pair of commas."},{"q":"Which sentence is punctuated correctly?","options":["The roads were icy, we drove slowly.","The roads were icy so, we drove slowly.","The roads were icy, so we drove, slowly.","The roads were icy, so we drove slowly."],"answer":3,"why":"A comma goes before so when it joins two full sentences."},{"q":"The ___ playground is closed for repairs.","options":["childrens","children's","childrens'","children'"],"answer":1,"why":"Children is already plural, so possession adds apostrophe plus s."}]},{"id":"G-17","topic":"Formal versus informal register","lesson":"Register is the level of formality. Formal writing, such as a complaint or a job application, uses full verb forms, precise vocabulary and complete sentences. Informal writing, such as a text to a friend, uses contractions, slang and short phrases. Choose a register that suits the reader and purpose, and stay consistent. Formal: I would like to request a refund. Informal: Can I get my money back?","items":[{"q":"Which sentence is most appropriate for an email to a landlord you do not know well?","options":["Hey, the heater's busted, fix it ASAP.","I am writing to report that the heater in my unit is not working.","The heater is totally dead, lol.","Your heater stuff is broken."],"answer":1,"why":"Only this sentence is polite, complete and precise."},{"q":"I would like to ___ a refund.","options":["get","grab","want","request"],"answer":3,"why":"Request is the formal verb for asking for something."},{"q":"Which closing is best for a formal letter?","options":["Cheers!","Later,","See ya","Sincerely,"],"answer":3,"why":"Sincerely is a standard formal closing."},{"q":"Which sentence suits a formal report?","options":["The team just didn't get it done.","The team couldn't finish on time.","The team flunked the deadline.","The team was unable to finish on time."],"answer":3,"why":"It avoids contractions and slang."},{"q":"Which sentence suits a casual text to a friend?","options":["I would be grateful if you could confirm your attendance.","Kindly advise whether you will be present.","Are you coming tonight?","Your attendance is requested."],"answer":2,"why":"Only the short, direct question sounds like friendly chat."},{"q":"Which phrase is the most formal?","options":["numerous individuals","a lot of people","loads of folks","tons of guys"],"answer":0,"why":"Numerous individuals is precise, neutral and formal."}]},{"id":"G-18","topic":"Softening and polite requests (hedging)","lesson":"English softens requests and opinions to sound polite or careful. Use could or would instead of can or will, and add please. Use indirect forms such as I was wondering if you could ... and Would you mind helping? Hedging words like perhaps, seems, may, might, tend to and a little reduce certainty. Claims about evidence are safer with suggest than with prove. Example: Could you send me the file? Example: The data suggests a slight rise.","items":[{"q":"Which is the most polite request?","options":["Give me the file.","Could you please send me the file?","I want the file now.","Send file."],"answer":1,"why":"Could plus please is a polite indirect request."},{"q":"Would you mind ___ the window?","options":["to close","close","closing","closed"],"answer":2,"why":"Would you mind is followed by a gerund."},{"q":"I was wondering ___ you could extend the deadline.","options":["that","what","do","if"],"answer":3,"why":"I was wondering if introduces a softened request."},{"q":"Which sentence softens a disagreement best?","options":["You are wrong about this.","That is a bad idea.","I see your point, but I wonder if another approach might work better.","No, that will never work."],"answer":2,"why":"It acknowledges the other view and uses wonder and might to soften."},{"q":"The early results ___ that the new schedule may reduce delays.","options":["prove","suggest","guarantee","insist"],"answer":1,"why":"Suggest is a cautious verb that fits the hedge may."},{"q":"Which refusal is the most tactful?","options":["I won't do that.","That's not my job.","No way.","I'm afraid I won't be able to take that on this week."],"answer":3,"why":"It apologizes and gives a limit rather than a flat refusal."}]},{"id":"G-19","topic":"Cohesion and pronoun reference","lesson":"Cohesion is how sentences connect. A pronoun such as it, they or this must clearly point to one earlier noun. If two nouns could fit, repeat the noun or rewrite. Use this or these with a noun to refer back to an idea: this trend. Use so, too and do for substitution: She likes tea, and so does he. Avoid switching pronouns without reason. Example: The tool broke, so we replaced it. Example: Sales rose; this growth helped.","items":[{"q":"'Maya told Priya that she had won.' The word she is unclear. Which revision fixes it?","options":["Maya told Priya that she herself had won, probably.","Maya told Priya, 'You have won the prize.'","Maya told her that she had won.","Maya told Priya that she won and was happy."],"answer":1,"why":"Direct speech with you removes the doubt about who won."},{"q":"The company launched a new app. ___ was praised by users.","options":["They","These","Them","It"],"answer":3,"why":"It refers clearly to the single app."},{"q":"Ravi enjoys hiking, and so ___ his sister.","options":["is","has","does","will"],"answer":2,"why":"So plus does substitutes for enjoys in the present simple."},{"q":"Prices rose sharply. ___ trend worried many shoppers.","options":["These","Those","Them","This"],"answer":3,"why":"This plus a singular noun refers back to the idea in the previous sentence."},{"q":"Which pair avoids needless repetition of 'the report'?","options":["The report was long. It was also confusing.","The report was long. They were also confusing.","The report was long. He was also confusing.","The report was long. Its was also confusing."],"answer":0,"why":"It matches the single noun report."},{"q":"In 'The tenants asked the landlord to fix the sink, but he refused,' who does he refer to?","options":["The tenants","The landlord","The sink","Nobody; it is unclear"],"answer":1,"why":"He is singular and male, so it can only refer to the landlord."}]},{"id":"G-20","topic":"Subject-verb agreement","lesson":"A verb must agree with its true subject, not with a nearby noun. Singular subjects take singular verbs (the list is long) and plural subjects take plural verbs. Phrases like of the items do not change the subject. With either/or and neither/nor, the verb agrees with the closer subject. Everyone, each and news are singular. Amounts of money or time are usually singular. Example: The box of pens is empty. Example: Neither Tim nor his friends were late.","items":[{"q":"The list of items ___ on the desk.","options":["are","were","have been","is"],"answer":3,"why":"The subject is the singular list, not items."},{"q":"Neither the manager nor the employees ___ aware of the change.","options":["was","is","has been","were"],"answer":3,"why":"The verb agrees with the nearer subject, employees."},{"q":"Everyone in the building ___ a badge.","options":["need","are needing","needs","have"],"answer":2,"why":"Everyone is singular."},{"q":"The news ___ good today.","options":["are","is","were","have been"],"answer":1,"why":"News is an uncountable noun that takes a singular verb."},{"q":"Fifty dollars ___ a fair price.","options":["is","are","were","have been"],"answer":0,"why":"A sum of money is treated as one amount."},{"q":"My colleagues and my manager ___ attending the seminar.","options":["is","are","was","has"],"answer":1,"why":"Two subjects joined by and take a plural verb."}]},{"id":"G-21","topic":"Parallel structure","lesson":"Parallel structure means items in a list or a pair use the same grammatical form. Match nouns with nouns, -ing forms with -ing forms, and infinitives with infinitives. Faulty: She likes swimming, to hike and cycling. Correct: She likes swimming, hiking and cycling. The same rule applies to pairs joined by both ... and, either ... or and not only ... but also. Repeat helper words consistently. Example: He is smart and kind.","items":[{"q":"She enjoys hiking, swimming and ___.","options":["to cycle","cycle","cycled","cycling"],"answer":3,"why":"The list uses -ing forms, so the last item must match."},{"q":"The job requires patience, accuracy and ___.","options":["organization","organized","to organize","organize"],"answer":0,"why":"The first two items are nouns, so a noun is needed."},{"q":"Which sentence is parallel?","options":["He likes to read, to write and swimming.","He likes reading, writing and to swim.","He likes reading, writing and swimming.","He likes to read, writing and to swim."],"answer":2,"why":"All three items are -ing forms."},{"q":"The new hire is both skilled ___ reliable.","options":["or","plus","and","with"],"answer":2,"why":"Both is paired with and."},{"q":"Not only did she finish early, but she also ___ the others.","options":["to help","helped","helping","help"],"answer":1,"why":"The second part matches the past tense of the first."},{"q":"The manual explains how to install the software, how to update it and ___.","options":["removal of it","removing it","to remove","how to remove it"],"answer":3,"why":"Each item begins with how to."}]},{"id":"G-22","topic":"Collocations","lesson":"Collocations are word pairs that native speakers use together naturally, and a different word sounds wrong even when the meaning is clear. We make a decision, a mistake, an effort and a reservation, but do homework, our best and a favour. We take a break and a breath, pay attention, and have a meeting. Rain is heavy, not strong. Learn verbs with their nouns. Example: make progress. Example: do the dishes.","items":[{"q":"Please ___ a decision by Friday.","options":["do","make","take","have"],"answer":1,"why":"Make a decision is the standard collocation."},{"q":"He ___ a mistake on the form.","options":["did","took","had","made"],"answer":3,"why":"We make mistakes, not do them."},{"q":"We need to ___ attention to the details.","options":["pay","make","put","do"],"answer":0,"why":"Pay attention is the fixed phrase."},{"q":"It was a ___ rain, so we stayed inside.","options":["strong","heavy","big","thick"],"answer":1,"why":"Rain is described as heavy."},{"q":"We should ___ our best to finish on time.","options":["make","take","do","put"],"answer":2,"why":"Do your best is the fixed phrase."},{"q":"She ___ a deep breath before the presentation.","options":["made","did","gave","took"],"answer":3,"why":"Take a breath is the usual collocation."}]},{"id":"G-23","topic":"Paraphrasing","lesson":"To paraphrase, restate an idea in different words and a different structure while keeping the meaning. Replace words with synonyms, change the word order, switch between active and passive, and swap a clause for a phrase. Do not change facts, add opinions or leave out key points. Original: The store closed because sales fell. Paraphrase: Falling sales forced the store to shut. Check that the logic of cause, contrast and result stays the same.","items":[{"q":"Original: 'The store reduced its prices because sales were falling.' Which is the best paraphrase?","options":["Sales fell because the store lowered prices.","The store lowered prices in response to declining sales.","The store raised prices when sales fell.","Falling prices caused the store to lose sales."],"answer":1,"why":"It keeps the same cause and effect in different words."},{"q":"Which phrase has the same meaning as 'in spite of the delay'?","options":["because of the delay","after the delay","despite the delay","owing to the delay"],"answer":2,"why":"Despite and in spite of both show contrast."},{"q":"Original: 'Few people attended the workshop.' Which sentence means the same?","options":["Only a small number of people attended the workshop.","Many people attended the workshop.","A crowd filled the workshop.","Nobody attended the workshop."],"answer":0,"why":"Few means a small number, not none and not many."},{"q":"Original: 'She is too tired to drive.' Which sentence means the same?","options":["She is tired enough to drive.","She is so tired that she cannot drive.","She is not tired, so she can drive.","She drives because she is tired."],"answer":1,"why":"Too tired to means so tired that it is not possible."},{"q":"Original: 'Unless you register, you cannot vote.' Which sentence means the same?","options":["If you register, you cannot vote.","If you don't register, you can vote.","You can vote whether or not you register.","You can vote only if you register."],"answer":3,"why":"Unless means if not, so registering is the only way to vote."},{"q":"Original: 'The chief executive's resignation surprised everyone.' Which is the best paraphrase?","options":["Nobody noticed when the chief executive resigned.","Everyone expected the chief executive to resign.","Everyone was surprised when the chief executive resigned.","The chief executive resigned because everyone was surprised."],"answer":2,"why":"It keeps the same event and the same reaction."}]},{"id":"G-24","topic":"Sentence variety","lesson":"Good writing mixes sentence types. A simple sentence has one clause. A compound sentence joins two equal clauses with and, but or so. A complex sentence adds a dependent clause beginning with words like because, although or when. Vary how sentences begin, using a clause, a participle phrase or an adverb, and combine short sentences to avoid a choppy rhythm. Example: Although it rained, we went out. Example: Walking home, I saw a fox.","items":[{"q":"Which sentence is complex?","options":["I called, and she answered.","I called her.","I called her because the meeting had changed.","Call me; I will answer."],"answer":2,"why":"It has a main clause and a dependent clause beginning with because."},{"q":"Combine: 'The bus was late. We missed the film.' Which is best?","options":["The bus was late we missed the film.","Because the bus was late, we missed the film.","The bus was late, we missed the film.","The bus was late because, we missed the film."],"answer":1,"why":"It joins the sentences with a clear reason and correct comma use."},{"q":"Which sentence begins with a participle phrase?","options":["Walking to work, I noticed a new bakery.","I noticed a new bakery while I walked.","A new bakery was noticed by me.","I noticed a new bakery, and I walked."],"answer":0,"why":"Walking to work is an -ing phrase that opens the sentence."},{"q":"Which sentence is compound?","options":["Although it rained, we went out.","The tall woman in red waved.","We were tired, but we finished the project.","Waving, she left."],"answer":2,"why":"Two full clauses are joined by the conjunction but."},{"q":"'I woke up. I ate. I left.' Which is the best way to combine these choppy sentences?","options":["I woke up, I ate, I left.","I woke up I ate and I left.","Waking up, ate and left.","After I woke up and ate, I left."],"answer":3,"why":"It joins the actions smoothly with correct grammar."},{"q":"Which sentence starts with an adverbial clause?","options":["The meeting ended at six, after running late.","I called the help desk, and they fixed it.","The help desk, which is on the second floor, is open.","Whenever the printer jams, I call the help desk."],"answer":3,"why":"Whenever the printer jams is a dependent clause placed first."}]}];
